[
    {
        "question": {
            "id": 8,
            "category": "debugging",
            "intent": "command_routing",
            "difficulty": "very_hard",
            "question": "A developer is debugging a LangGraph application where a node uses `Command` to update state and route execution to another node. The state update appears to succeed, but after the graph is resumed from a checkpoint, the workflow follows an unexpected path. Review the implementation and determine whether the problem is caused by `Command` semantics, checkpoint restoration, or graph routing. Explain: 1. How `Command` combines state updates with control flow. 2. How the destination node is determined when `goto` is specified. 3. What happens when a node returns `Command(goto=...)` and that same node also has a statically defined outgoing edge using `add_edge(...)`. 4. Whether `Command(goto=...)` overrides the statically defined edge or whether both routing mechanisms apply. 5. How this explains the observed behavior where `finish` can be reached without executing `execute`. 6. Whether the shown `Command(resume=True)` is actually appropriate for the provided example, given that the code does not show an `interrupt()`. 7. Recommend the correct graph construction.",
            "expected_files": [
                "command.mdx",
                "graph-api.mdx",
                "use-graph-api.mdx",
                "persistence.mdx",
                "checkpoint-sqlite/langgraph/checkpoint/sqlite/aio.py",
                "langgraph/langgraph/types.py",
                "langgraph/langgraph/graph/state.py",
                "prebuilt/langgraph/prebuilt/tool_node.py"
            ],
            "concepts": [
                "Command",
                "state updates",
                "control flow",
                "goto routing",
                "dynamic edges",
                "static edges",
                "graph routing",
                "checkpoint restoration",
                "interrupt",
                "resume"
            ],
            "tags": [
                "command_goto",
                "dynamic_routing",
                "static_edge_conflict",
                "command_resume",
                "interrupt_resume",
                "graph_control_flow",
                "checkpointing"
            ],
            "code_snippet": "class State(TypedDict):\n    status: str\n    result: str\n\ndef review(state: State):\n    if state[\"status\"] == \"approved\":\n        return Command(\n            update={\"result\": \"approved\"},\n            goto=\"execute\"\n        )\n\n    return Command(\n        update={\"result\": \"rejected\"},\n        goto=\"cancel\"\n    )\n\nbuilder = StateGraph(State)\n\nbuilder.add_node(\"review\", review)\nbuilder.add_node(\"execute\", execute)\nbuilder.add_node(\"cancel\", cancel)\nbuilder.add_node(\"finish\", finish)\n\nbuilder.add_edge(START, \"review\")\nbuilder.add_edge(\"review\", \"finish\")\nbuilder.add_edge(\"execute\", \"finish\")\nbuilder.add_edge(\"cancel\", \"finish\")\n\ngraph = builder.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)\n\nconfig = {\n    \"configurable\": {\n        \"thread_id\": \"review-123\"\n    }\n}\n\nresult = graph.invoke(\n    {\"status\": \"approved\", \"result\": \"\"},\n    config=config\n)\n\n# Later, after an application restart:\nresult = graph.invoke(\n    Command(resume=True),\n    config=config\n)",
            "observed_behavior": "`review` returns `Command(goto=\"execute\", update=...)`. The state update is persisted correctly. After recovery, the workflow sometimes reaches `finish` without executing `execute`. Removing `builder.add_edge(\"review\", \"finish\")` appears to change the behavior.",
            "expected_behavior": "`Command(goto=\"execute\")` should dynamically route execution to `execute`, while the `review -> finish` static edge also remains active because `Command` adds dynamic routing rather than overriding static edges. Therefore both `execute` and `finish` can be scheduled from `review`. The graph should use either `Command(goto=...)` or a static edge for routing from `review`, not both. Additionally, `Command(resume=True)` is not appropriate for the shown example because no `interrupt()` is present.",
            "error": null,
            "logs": null,
            "evidence": [
                {
                    "id": 1,
                    "title": "Command implementation",
                    "content": "`Command` is documented as a primitive for updating graph state and sending messages to nodes. Its parameters include `update`, `resume`, `goto`, and `graph=None`. `update` applies a state update, `resume` is used to resume execution with a value and is intended to be used together with `interrupt()`, and `goto` specifies a destination node, sequence of nodes, or `Send` object."
                },
                {
                    "id": 2,
                    "title": "Command and dynamic/static routing",
                    "content": "The LangGraph Graph API documentation states that `Command` only adds dynamic edges. Static edges defined with `add_edge` still execute. If a node returns `Command(goto=\"my_other_node\")` and the graph also contains `graph.add_edge(\"node_a\", \"node_b\")`, then both `node_b` and `my_other_node` run. The documentation recommends using either `Command` or static edges to route from a node, rather than both."
                },
                {
                    "id": 3,
                    "title": "Command update and routing",
                    "content": "The Graph API documentation demonstrates that a node can return `Command(update={\"foo\": \"bar\"}, goto=\"my_other_node\")`, combining a state update with control flow."
                },
                {
                    "id": 4,
                    "title": "Resume semantics",
                    "content": "The LangGraph interrupt documentation shows that `Command(resume=...)` is used to resume execution after an `interrupt()`. The value supplied to `resume` becomes the return value of the paused `interrupt()` call. The supplied code contains no `interrupt()`."
                }
            ]
        },
