        "generation_metrics": {
            "answer": "The behavior is caused by **graph routing**, not by the state update or, based on the provided evidence, by checkpoint restoration.\n\n### 1. `Command` combines state updates and control flow\n\nA node can return both:\n\n```python\nCommand(\n    update={\"result\": \"approved\"},\n    goto=\"execute\",\n)\n```\n\n`update` applies the state change, while `goto` dynamically sends execution to the specified destination. These are independent parts of the same command.\n\nThus, in the approved branch:\n\n- `result` is updated to `\"approved\"`.\n- `execute` is selected as a dynamic destination.\n\n### 2. Destination selected by `goto`\n\n`goto=\"execute\"` specifies the node named `execute` as the dynamic destination. `goto` can also specify multiple destinations or a `Send` value, according to the `Command` API.\n\n### 3. Interaction with a static outgoing edge\n\nThe graph also contains:\n\n```python\nbuilder.add_edge(\"review\", \"finish\")\n```\n\nAccording to the Graph API behavior in the evidence, dynamic routing from `Command(goto=...)` **adds** a route; it does not remove or replace statically defined outgoing edges.\n\nTherefore, when `review` returns:\n\n```python\nCommand(\n    update={\"result\": \"approved\"},\n    goto=\"execute\",\n)\n```\n\nboth of these routes apply:\n\n```text\nreview -> execute   # dynamic route from Command\nreview -> finish    # static route from add_edge\n```\n\n### 4. Does `goto` override the static edge?\n\nNo. Both routing mechanisms apply.\n\nThe effective graph includes:\n\n```text\nSTART -> review\nreview -> execute    # from goto\nreview -> finish     # static edge\nexecute -> finish\ncancel -> finish\n```\n\nThe recommended design is to use either dynamic routing with `Command` or static edges for a node's routing, rather than combining both.\n\n### 5. Why `finish` can be reached without `execute`\n\nBecause `review -> finish` remains active, `finish` can be scheduled directly from `review`. It is not dependent on `execute` completing first.\n\nConsequently, the graph may reach `finish` through the static route even though `review` also dynamically routes to `execute`. The evidence does not establish a specific scheduling order, but it does establish that both destinations can run. This explains why removing:\n\n```python\nbuilder.add_edge(\"review\", \"finish\")\n```\n\nchanges the behavior.\n\nThe state update being persisted correctly is consistent with `Command(update=...)` working as intended. The unexpected path is explained by the additional static edge.\n\n### 6. Is `Command(resume=True)` appropriate here?\n\nNo, not for the shown example.\n\n`Command(resume=...)` is intended to resume execution after a node has called `interrupt()`. The supplied code does not show an `interrupt()` call:\n\n```python\ndef review(state: State):\n    ...\n```\n\nThe interrupt documentation states that the value supplied to `resume` becomes the return value of the paused `interrupt()` call. Since no interrupt is present, `Command(resume=True)` is not appropriate for this workflow. The provided context does not determine the exact runtime behavior of invoking `resume` when no interrupt is pending, but it is not the documented usage.\n\n### 7. Recommended graph construction\n\nSince `review` already performs conditional routing with `Command(goto=...)`, remove its static outgoing edge:\n\n```python\nbuilder.add_edge(START, \"review\")\nbuilder.add_node(\"review\", review)\nbuilder.add_node(\"execute\", execute)\nbuilder.add_node(\"cancel\", cancel)\nbuilder.add_node(\"finish\", finish)\n\nbuilder.add_edge(\"execute\", \"finish\")\nbuilder.add_edge(\"cancel\", \"finish\")\n```\n\nKeep the node implementation:\n\n```python\ndef review(state: State):\n    if state[\"status\"] == \"approved\":\n        return Command(\n            update={\"result\": \"approved\"},\n            goto=\"execute\",\n        )\n\n    return Command(\n        update={\"result\": \"rejected\"},\n        goto=\"cancel\",\n    )\n```\n\nThen the intended paths are:\n\n```text\napproved -> review -> execute -> finish\nrejected -> review -> cancel -> finish\n```\n\nFor the shown code, the initial invocation should provide the ordinary graph input. A `Command(resume=...)` invocation should only be used if the workflow actually paused at an `interrupt()`.",
